From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 09:47:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: avd: decode H.264 in frame-based mode

Chromium's V4L2 stateless H.264 decoder sets the decode mode to
FRAME_BASED, prefixes each slice with an Annex-B start code, puts all
of a picture's slices into one OUTPUT buffer and sends no slice
parameters. The driver offered slice-based mode only, so Chromium could
not decode H.264 on the AVD at all.

Offer V4L2_STATELESS_H264_DECODE_MODE_FRAME_BASED and
V4L2_STATELESS_H264_START_CODE_ANNEX_B, keeping SLICE_BASED and NONE as
the defaults, so clients that use the defaults are unchanged. In
frame-based mode, split the buffer at its start codes and read each
slice header (H.264 7.3.3, frame pictures only, which the driver already
requires): the fields the instruction builder uses, the prediction weight
table, and header_bit_size counted the way avd_h264_find_slice_data()
expects. Build the final reference lists from the v4l2-h264 initial
lists and the slice's ref_pic_list_modification() (8.2.4.3), then queue
each slice through the existing per-slice path and submit the picture
once.

Chromium also states the picture format before any stream data with an
SPS that carries only the chroma format and bit depths (profile_idc 0,
no frame_mbs_only_flag). try_ctrl and start() refused it as interlaced.
Check such an SPS for its format fields only; every picture's SPS is
still checked in full before it is decoded.

KUnit: the header reader on real x264 slices (CABAC and CAVLC, I/P/B,
list modification, weights, emulation prevention), synthetic slices
(chroma weights, unsupported and malformed headers), the list
modification (wrap, long-term, errors) and the start-code splitter.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
(cherry picked from commit eec68d9b9412a53310637b90945915d533d8a757)
